{
  "slug": "erdos-81-oq-01",
  "title": "Improving the Upper Bound for Clique Partitions of Chordal Graphs",
  "phase": "OBSERVE",
  "status": "active",
  "tier": "C",
  "path": "full",
  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "Let f(n) = max { cp(G) : G chordal, |V(G)| = n }, where cp(G) is the minimum number of cliques needed to partition E(G). The Erdős–Ordman–Zalcstein bound gives f(n) ≤ (1/4 − ε)n², while the split graph K_{n/3} ∪ complement(K_{2n/3}) forces f(n) ≥ n²/6 + O(n). Conjecture: f(n) = n²/6 + O(n); equivalently, improve the upper bound to f(n) ≤ n²/6 + O(n).",
    "plain": "A chordal graph is one where every cycle of four or more vertices has a chord. We cover its edges with cliques so each edge is used exactly once, and ask how few cliques are ever needed in the worst case. Some chordal graphs require about n²/6 cliques, and none need more than about n²/4. The conjecture is that the true worst case is n²/6 — pushing the upper bound down from n²/4 to n²/6.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "Pins down the leading constant for clique partitions of an entire natural graph class (chordal graphs).",
      "Connects to sparse matrix factorization, elimination trees, and confluent graph drawing.",
      "Closing the 1/4 vs 1/6 gap is the headline open case of Erdős Problem #81."
    ]
  },
